> djer:
>         Well, it becomes another story with the introduction of the "cu
> broda".  In my sentence it is just "[ro] lo ci nanmu ku goi da..." The
> predication with pencu is with that second order "da" which is equated
> to "[ro] lo ci nanmu ku".  The parser output demonstrates that the "goi
> da" makes this assignment.

The construction is grammatical, it just doesn't make that much sense.
{da} is a bindable variable (like usually "x" in logic), while {goi} is
used to assign values to fixed variables (like usually "a" in logic).
You are mixing the two things and I'm not sure what you mean by it.

> There is no x or da that stands for one
> individual object in my sentence.

Then using {goi} doesn't make sense. It is used to assign individuals
to fixed variables like {ko'a}.

